<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mengu-Timur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1280</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Möngke Temür</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mengu-Timur was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1266 or 1267 until 1280, a grandson of Batu. He struck coins in his own name, marking the Horde's effective independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, and granted trading privileges to the Genoese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>. He continued the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and issued charters exempting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> from tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mengu-timur.jpg" alt="Mengu-Timur" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1405-1409 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mengu-Timur_portrait._Timurid_genealogy,_1405-1409,_Samarkand_(Topkapi_Sarayi_M%C3%BCzesi,_H2152)_folio_36b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mengu-Timur, or Möngke Temür, was a son of Toqoqan Khan, himself a son of Batu, and of Köchu Khatun of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a>. His name means Eternal Iron in Mongolian. He was one of three candidates for the throne after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a>, the others being Berke's son and his own younger brother, Tode Mongke. Some sources say Berke had designated him as heir to secure the support of Batu's followers. The succession caused tension within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, which Russian sources reflect, and after several months the Jochid elites chose him as khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At the start of his reign Mengu-Timur reformed the monetary system with new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins that bore his name and seal, with no reference to the great khan. He refused to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> on his enthronement and instead required an ambassador of Kublai to come to him. He increased the number of mints and made coins of one region valid across the Horde, which protected local trade.</p><p>His first decree, in 1267, exempted the Russian Church from tax, and Russian Orthodox clergy were also freed from military service. This followed the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> of granting priests tarkhan status in exchange for prayers for the khan and his family. He did not convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but he exchanged embassies with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and the alliance continued after the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> ended. Around 1275-1280 he allowed the Genoese to rent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>, welcoming the traders as long as they paid taxes and respected the law.</p><p>He tried to restore peaceful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Michael VIII Palaiologos sent a large tribute of textiles and offered his daughter Euphrosyne, who was married to Nogai Khan. The agreement that let ships pass through the Bosporus and the Dardanelles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> was maintained. In the wider Mongol world he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a>, a rival of Kublai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1280 Mengu-Timur launched a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> that ended in defeat, and he died soon afterwards. His alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>'s control of access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, while the alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochids</a> access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mengu-Timur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Until the 1260s the khans acknowledged the overlordship of the Great Khan in Mongolia; <b>Mengu-Timur</b> (r. 1266–1280) was the first to strike coins in his own name, marking the khanate's effective independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
